Iteration #1 done — read the QuantStart guide, designed 3 experiments around its core warnings (data-snooping bias, data cleanliness, cost realism), ran them, and updated `~/quant/REPORT.md`.

## What the article emphasized → what I tested
1. **Optimization/data-snooping bias** → parameter sweeps with a strict train(2012–18)/test(2019–26) split
2. **Data accuracy/cleanliness** → rolling z-score spike filter on all price series
3. **Transaction-cost realism** → 0 / 10 / 30 bps stress test

## Key results

**E1 — Data check:** 0 bad ticks across all 9 tickers; the Yahoo data is clean, no corrections needed.

**E2 — SMA window sweep (the money lesson):**

| Window | In-sample Sharpe | Out-of-sample Sharpe |
|---|---|---|
| 150 (best IS) | 0.37 | 0.62 |
| 200 | 0.13 | 0.57 |
| 300 | 0.19 | 0.61 |

In-sample rankings are pure noise — the "best" window in-sample has no special edge out-of-sample. Tuning parameters on the full sample would have been textbook data-snooping. (Chart: `iter1_sma_heatmap.png`)

**E3 — Momentum lookback sweep:** same story; IS–OOS Sharpe correlation is essentially zero (`iter1_momentum_oos.png`, `iter1_momentum_sweep.csv`).

**E4 — Cost sensitivity (brutal but honest):**

| Strategy | 0 bps | 10 bps | 30 bps |
|---|---|---|---|
| SMA200 Trend | 0.63 | 0.38 | **-0.12** |
| Dual Momentum | 0.62 | 0.33 | **-0.26** |

At retail-level costs (~10 bps) the edges are thin; at 30 bps both flip negative. Execution quality is a first-order input, exactly as the article argues.

## Also: a real bug found and fixed
The engine wasn't **summing** multi-asset transaction costs (they were applied per-asset and silently diluted). Fixed in `engine.py` — this means the Round-2 multi-asset numbers from earlier were slightly overstated; I flagged that in the report header.

## New files in `~/quant/`
`run_iteration1.py`, `iter1_spike_check.csv`, `iter1_sma_sweep.csv`, `iter1_momentum_sweep.csv`, `iter1_cost_sensitivity.csv`, `iter1_sma_heatmap.png`, `iter1_momentum_oos.png`, and the appended `REPORT.md` section.

Next iteration candidates (from the article's remaining themes): survivorship-bias-aware universes, Kelly-style position sizing, and an execution-system layer with slippage modeling. Want me to run iteration #2?
